WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.934
Welcome to Module 4 of Defender
X D R Engineering and Operations:

2
00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:08.712
hunting, detections as
code, and identity signals.

3
00:00:08.712 --> 00:00:12.327
A detection that lives only in
a portal can't be reviewed,

4
00:00:12.327 --> 00:00:16.963
compared or rolled back, and one that
can't be shown to fire isn't a control.

5
00:00:16.963 --> 00:00:20.816
In this module you learn to keep
custom detections as files,

6
00:00:20.816 --> 00:00:25.220
test each one against data it must
find and data it must stay quiet in,

7
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.737
and deploy it through Microsoft Graph.

8
00:00:27.737 --> 00:00:30.289
Then you join the identity and cloud-app

9
00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:33.150
sensors to the endpoint's
alerts, in one query.

10
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:38.562
The module covers Steps 7 and 8 of the
Defender X D R implementation guide.

11
00:00:38.562 --> 00:00:43.310
It takes about forty-five minutes,
plus Lab 2 in your own test tenant.

12
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.572
Why detections as code?

13
00:00:48.572 --> 00:00:52.047
A rule that lives only in a
portal has three problems.

14
00:00:52.047 --> 00:00:54.185
No one reviews it before it runs.

15
00:00:54.185 --> 00:00:57.123
No one can compare what's
deployed with what was approved.

16
00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:58.848
And there's no clean way back.

17
00:00:58.848 --> 00:01:01.435
A rule kept as a file fixes all three.

18
00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:06.360
It's reviewed, deployed disabled, and
enabled by a reviewed change to the file.

19
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.445
It's tested against a
known-bad event it must find,

20
00:01:09.445 --> 00:01:12.222
and a known-good window
it must stay quiet in.

21
00:01:12.222 --> 00:01:15.972
And a weekly export compares what's
deployed with the repository.

22
00:01:15.972 --> 00:01:20.227
The lifecycle itself, author, test,
alert-only, promote and review,

23
00:01:20.227 --> 00:01:25.768
comes from the Security Operations and
Telemetry book's Chapter 11, which also says

24
00:01:25.768 --> 00:01:31.147
a rule that reads only Defender tables is
written as a Defender custom detection.

25
00:01:31.147 --> 00:01:34.273
This module puts those
detections in a repository

26
00:01:34.273 --> 00:01:36.654
and deploys them through the Graph A P I.

27
00:01:36.654 --> 00:01:39.333
Rules that correlate across other sources

28
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.510
stay as Sentinel analytics
rules, in that book.

29
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.173
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

30
00:01:49.173 --> 00:01:54.260
Write a custom detection as a repository
file, and say why it starts disabled.

31
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.948
Test a rule against a validation window,
and explain the thirty-day limit.

32
00:01:58.948 --> 00:02:04.029
Deploy a rule through the Graph detection
rules A P I, and use the portal,

33
00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:08.585
from the same file, where the A P
I's availability isn't specified.

34
00:02:08.585 --> 00:02:11.773
Name Defender for
Identity's and Defender for

35
00:02:11.773 --> 00:02:14.710
Cloud Apps' G C C gaps,
and their interim path.

36
00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.141
And run the cross-source
query that finds a

37
00:02:17.141 --> 00:02:19.572
user with alerts from
more than one source.

38
00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:20.997
You need Module 3.

39
00:02:20.997 --> 00:02:26.598
Skim the rule lifecycle in the Security
Operations and Telemetry book's Chapter 11.

40
00:02:26.598 --> 00:02:30.260
Lab 2 runs in a test
tenant, never production.

41
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.897
Unit 1 is the rule file.

42
00:02:35.897 --> 00:02:41.119
It follows the example shape on Microsoft
Learn's page for creating a detection

43
00:02:41.119 --> 00:02:45.865
rule: the type, a short client-chosen I
D, a display name, the status, the query,

44
00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:49.266
a schedule, and the alert
template, with its severity,

45
00:02:49.266 --> 00:02:52.035
entity mappings and the attack technique.

46
00:02:52.035 --> 00:02:56.565
The guide's example looks for
encoded PowerShell launched by Word,

47
00:02:56.565 --> 00:02:58.460
Excel or Outlook, every hour.

48
00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.685
Four rules apply.

49
00:02:59.685 --> 00:03:03.535
The file starts with status
disabled, until its test passes.

50
00:03:03.535 --> 00:03:06.231
Automated actions such
as isolate are left out,

51
00:03:06.231 --> 00:03:09.135
because containment stays
on the response ladder.

52
00:03:09.135 --> 00:03:14.385
Enabling a rule is a change to the status
in the file, reviewed and redeployed.

53
00:03:14.385 --> 00:03:17.360
And rollback sets the
status back to disabled.

54
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.896
Learn says deploying needs the Custom
Detection dot Read Write dot All permission,

55
00:03:22.896 --> 00:03:26.266
and a role the A P I accepts:
Detection tuning, manage,

56
00:03:26.266 --> 00:03:30.598
in unified role-based access
control, or Security Administrator.

57
00:03:30.598 --> 00:03:36.010
The Graph security A P I's quota is
ten new rules a minute per application.

58
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.095
Every rule has a validation dataset,

59
00:03:42.095 --> 00:03:45.285
and a rule that can't be
shown to fire isn't finished.

60
00:03:45.285 --> 00:03:48.540
The known-bad half is an
event the rule must find,

61
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:53.048
from the guide's synthetic events
or a replay on a ring zero device.

62
00:03:53.048 --> 00:03:56.732
The known-good half is a window
the rule must stay quiet in,

63
00:03:56.732 --> 00:03:58.535
or stay under its threshold.

64
00:03:58.535 --> 00:04:02.560
Then schema governance, the
program's risk-based recommendation.

65
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.971
Each rule file names the
tables and columns it reads,

66
00:04:05.971 --> 00:04:11.048
and a weekly review runs each rule's query
and fails it when a column is renamed.

67
00:04:11.048 --> 00:04:15.747
Microsoft Learn keeps a list of naming
changes in the advanced hunting schema.

68
00:04:15.747 --> 00:04:17.598
One limit shapes all of this.

69
00:04:17.598 --> 00:04:23.135
Advanced hunting keeps thirty days, so a
validation window is at most thirty days.

70
00:04:23.135 --> 00:04:28.863
Learn's page for the run hunting query A
P I says it defaults to thirty days, and

71
00:04:28.863 --> 00:04:34.780
takes an I S O eighty-six oh one time span,
such as P thirty D, which the lab uses.

72
00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:43.368
Deploying is a post of the file to the
detection rules endpoint, then a read-back.

73
00:04:43.368 --> 00:04:48.256
Validation is an export of everything
deployed, compared with the repository.

74
00:04:48.256 --> 00:04:52.647
The guide's pass: every rule in the
repository is deployed with the same

75
00:04:52.647 --> 00:04:57.267
query text and status, and nothing is
deployed that isn't in the repository.

76
00:04:57.267 --> 00:04:57.851
Now G C C.

77
00:04:57.851 --> 00:05:02.901
Microsoft Learn's run hunting
query page lists it as available

78
00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:07.368
on the global service, the
worldwide Graph host G C C uses.

79
00:05:07.368 --> 00:05:11.416
Learn's detection rules pages
have no national-cloud table,

80
00:05:11.416 --> 00:05:16.044
so the guide records the A P I's G C
C availability as not yet specified,

81
00:05:16.044 --> 00:05:19.267
and lists it among the
things it couldn't verify.

82
00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:24.255
The interim path is to create the rule in
the portal from the same repository file.

83
00:05:24.255 --> 00:05:26.764
The file stays the source
of truth either way,

84
00:05:26.764 --> 00:05:30.040
and the deployed-rules check
still compares the two.

85
00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.877
Unit 2 is identity and cloud-app signals.

86
00:05:36.877 --> 00:05:41.164
Defender for Identity's sensors go
on domain controllers, A D F S,

87
00:05:41.164 --> 00:05:46.605
A D C S and Entra Connect servers, and
Defender for Cloud Apps' connectors bring in

88
00:05:46.605 --> 00:05:52.128
software-as-a-service behavior, so both
land in the same incidents as the endpoint.

89
00:05:52.128 --> 00:05:55.896
Pilot the sensors on one domain
controller per domain first;

90
00:05:55.896 --> 00:05:59.590
domain controllers remain Tier
zero until they're retired.

91
00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:04.443
Microsoft Learn's government page
for Defender for Identity gives

92
00:06:04.443 --> 00:06:08.690
the sensor endpoint for G C C, a
G C C address, or the service tag.

93
00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:13.966
Learn also says new features reach G C C
within ninety days of commercial release,

94
00:06:13.966 --> 00:06:16.565
and previews may not be supported there.

95
00:06:16.565 --> 00:06:17.678
Then the gaps.

96
00:06:17.678 --> 00:06:22.467
Learn's Cloud Apps government page
lists App Governance's predefined

97
00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:27.090
policy alerts and threat-detection
alerts as roadmap items for G C C.

98
00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.286
And Learn's advanced
hunting schema says Cloud

99
00:06:30.286 --> 00:06:32.990
Apps behaviors aren't available for G C C.

100
00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:38.325
The interim path for O auth app abuse is
the application detections in Sentinel,

101
00:06:38.325 --> 00:06:41.840
in the Security Operations
and Telemetry book.

102
00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:50.278
Lab 2 runs the guide's own commands
in a test tenant, never production.

103
00:06:50.278 --> 00:06:53.277
The authors haven't run
them, as the page says.

104
00:06:53.277 --> 00:06:58.115
You need Module 1's test tenant,
in the worldwide or G C C cloud.

105
00:06:58.115 --> 00:07:03.106
You need an account with Detection
tuning, manage, or Security Administrator,

106
00:07:03.106 --> 00:07:06.459
activated through pim, and
Defender for Identity or

107
00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:09.578
Cloud Apps connected, for
step six to return rows.

108
00:07:09.578 --> 00:07:13.640
Step one connects with the hunting
and custom detection permissions.

109
00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.752
Step two writes the guide's example rule
to a file, with its status disabled.

110
00:07:18.752 --> 00:07:23.715
The slide shortens it; the page has the
whole file, exactly as the guide gives it.

111
00:07:23.715 --> 00:07:28.677
Step three reads the file back and runs
its query against the last thirty days,

112
00:07:28.677 --> 00:07:31.390
saving the output as
the rule's test record.

113
00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.515
In a quiet test tenant it returns no rows.

114
00:07:34.515 --> 00:07:36.815
That shows the known-good window only.

115
00:07:36.815 --> 00:07:40.970
A rule isn't finished until its
known-bad event is shown to fire.

116
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.520
Step four is the lab's only
write: it posts the file to the

117
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:52.658
detection rules endpoint, then
reads the rule back by its I D.

118
00:07:52.658 --> 00:07:55.218
It should come back with the file's query

119
00:07:55.218 --> 00:07:57.857
text and status disabled,
so it never runs.

120
00:07:57.857 --> 00:08:02.260
Step five exports everything deployed,
which is the guide's validate step:

121
00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.374
every rule in the repository deployed
with the same query and status,

122
00:08:06.374 --> 00:08:09.333
and nothing deployed that
isn't in the repository.

123
00:08:09.333 --> 00:08:12.108
Step six is the guide's
cross-source query.

124
00:08:12.108 --> 00:08:16.479
It takes the user entities in the
last seven days of alert evidence,

125
00:08:16.479 --> 00:08:20.850
joins the alert info, and counts,
for each user, the distinct service

126
00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:24.983
sources that raised alerts, keeping
users seen by more than one.

127
00:08:24.983 --> 00:08:27.152
An identity seen by the endpoint,

128
00:08:27.152 --> 00:08:30.769
identity and cloud-app sensors
together is a priority.

129
00:08:30.769 --> 00:08:35.707
The guide's pass: the query runs and returns
rows from more than one service source

130
00:08:35.707 --> 00:08:40.570
across the fleet, showing the identity
and cloud-app sources are feeding alerts.

131
00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:43.595
A test tenant with few
alerts may return none.

132
00:08:43.595 --> 00:08:45.220
Step seven backs it out.

133
00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.210
The rule is deployed disabled,

134
00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:51.620
which is the guide's rollback
state; leave it so, and disconnect.

135
00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:58.682
The federal requirement, from
the guide's requirements table.

136
00:08:58.682 --> 00:09:02.473
Gate seven supports S I four,
system monitoring; A U six,

137
00:09:02.473 --> 00:09:08.417
enhancements one and three, automated
process integration and correlating audit

138
00:09:08.417 --> 00:09:10.829
record repositories; and C M three,

139
00:09:10.829 --> 00:09:14.620
configuration change control,
here for rule changes.

140
00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:19.907
The evidence is the rule repository, the
test outputs and the deployed-rules export.

141
00:09:19.907 --> 00:09:26.084
Gate eight supports S I four; A U six,
enhancement three, correlating repositories;

142
00:09:26.084 --> 00:09:31.432
and A C two, enhancement twelve,
account monitoring for atypical usage.

143
00:09:31.432 --> 00:09:35.532
The evidence is the correlation
query output and the sensors' health.

144
00:09:35.532 --> 00:09:40.245
All from NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three, revision five.

145
00:09:40.245 --> 00:09:44.351
Each row lines up with fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicators,

146
00:09:44.351 --> 00:09:49.620
which bind cloud service providers; the
agency lines its own evidence up with them.

147
00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.710
The gates support these controls,

148
00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:55.280
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

149
00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:01.005
Check yourself with two questions.

150
00:10:01.005 --> 00:10:03.980
First: an auditor asks
for a rule's validation

151
00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.955
against the last ninety
days of hunting data.

152
00:10:06.955 --> 00:10:07.980
What's the limit?

153
00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.205
The answer is b, thirty days.

154
00:10:10.205 --> 00:10:12.672
Advanced hunting keeps thirty days,

155
00:10:12.672 --> 00:10:17.442
and Microsoft Learn says the run
hunting query A P I defaults to thirty.

156
00:10:17.442 --> 00:10:22.217
That's also why the monthly evidence
run in Module 5 files its results.

157
00:10:22.217 --> 00:10:24.995
Second: a G C C agency
needs the O auth app

158
00:10:24.995 --> 00:10:28.205
abuse alerts that App
Governance would give.

159
00:10:28.205 --> 00:10:29.693
What's the interim path?

160
00:10:29.693 --> 00:10:33.488
The answer is b: the application
detections in Sentinel,

161
00:10:33.488 --> 00:10:38.192
because Learn lists App Governance's
alerts as roadmap items for G C C.

162
00:10:38.192 --> 00:10:43.460
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

163
00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:48.035
To sum up.

164
00:10:48.035 --> 00:10:50.029
You wrote a detection as a file,

165
00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:53.460
in Microsoft Learn's shape,
with its status disabled.

166
00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:57.263
You tested its query against a
thirty-day validation window,

167
00:10:57.263 --> 00:10:59.697
and saved the result as its test record.

168
00:10:59.697 --> 00:11:02.047
You deployed it through
Graph, read it back,

169
00:11:02.047 --> 00:11:05.135
and compared everything
deployed with the repository.

170
00:11:05.135 --> 00:11:07.644
And you ran the cross-source
query that finds

171
00:11:07.644 --> 00:11:10.085
a user with alerts from
more than one source.

172
00:11:10.085 --> 00:11:12.994
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

173
00:11:12.994 --> 00:11:17.761
the detection repository at a tagged
commit, each rule's test output, the

174
00:11:17.761 --> 00:11:21.316
deployed-rules export, the
correlation query output,

175
00:11:21.316 --> 00:11:23.497
and the identity sensors' health.

176
00:11:23.497 --> 00:11:27.842
Next is Module 5, the last:
automation with approval boundaries,

177
00:11:27.842 --> 00:11:31.490
the operating cadence, and
the monthly evidence run.
